-#
-#  How the CONDENSED bookings table sizes its columns -- the compact variant
-#  embedded in a Buchhaltung item's detail (fin/bookings/_bookings_table with
-#  condensed: true). The widget only says that a condensed table lays out by
-#  content and does not wrap (shared/wsjrdp/_expandable_table_styles); which
-#  column may wrap and how wide the short ones are is a property of THIS
-#  dataset, so it is declared here.
-#
-#  The intent, in three rules:
-#  * from 700px of CONTAINER width the table switches to table-layout: fixed and
-#    the percentages below act like flex weights -- every short column grows a
-#    little with the box, the description (the only widthless column) takes what
-#    is left over, the largest share and more so the longer its text. The
-#    percentages reserve space for the short columns, so the table always fits;
-#  * the description wraps. It has to: a fixed layout does not clip, so the
-#    widget's nowrap default would run the text straight across the code columns
-#    to its right;
-#  * below 460px the box is too tight even for content-sized columns, so the
-#    code columns drop out (they are still on the full Buchungen page) and
-#    Datum / Betrag / Beschreibung stay readable without side-scrolling.
-#
-#  Widths are relative to the surrounding BOX, not the viewport (@container):
-#  the detail card is far narrower than the window, so viewport breakpoints
-#  would not match. The scrolling wrappers are declared as size containers in
-#  the widget's styles.
-#
-#  Each selector addresses one column of Fin::DatevBookingsColumns through the
-#  css class the widget puts on its cells: "<css_prefix>-<key>", the prefix of
-#  that column set being "bkcol". So every class below carries a column KEY, and
-#  a renamed key has to be renamed here too -- which is what
-#  spec/views/fin/bookings/condensed_styles_spec.rb checks.
-#
-#  Rendered from fin/bookings/_bookings_table whenever it renders condensed, and
-#  AFTER the table, so this block always follows the widget's own <style>: both
-#  declare table-layout on .bookings-table-condensed, with the same specificity,
-#  so only the document order makes the fixed layout win over the widget's auto.
-#  The guard emits the <style> only once per request (same pattern as
-#  shared/wsjrdp/_moss_kind_styles).
- return if @fin_bookings_condensed_styles_emitted
- @fin_bookings_condensed_styles_emitted = true
:css
  /* Default (narrow to intermediate boxes): the description is the flexible
     "leftover" column -- it shrinks and wraps so every other column stays
     visible and the table fits its box, however little room there is. This is
     the common case; only a very wide or a very narrow box overrides it. */
  .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-signed_base_amount { padding-right: 1rem; }
  .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-posting_text { white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* Plenty of room: a fixed layout whose percentages act like flex weights, so every
     column grows a little and the description (the leftover) grows the most. */
  @container (min-width: 700px) {
    .bookings-table-condensed { table-layout: fixed; }
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-booking_date { width: 12%; }
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-signed_base_amount { width: 13%; }
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-cost_center_number { width: 8%; }
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-account_number { width: 9%; }
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-offsetting_account_number { width: 10%; }
  }
  /* Too tight even for content-sized columns (phones, very narrow windows): drop the
     code columns -- still on the full bookings page -- so Datum / Betrag /
     Beschreibung stay readable without side-scrolling. */
  @container (max-width: 460px) {
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-cost_center_number,
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-secondary_cost_center_number,
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-account_number,
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-offsetting_account_number { display: none; }
    .bookings-table-condensed .bkcol-posting_text { min-width: 7rem; }
  }
